\lessonlabel{1}
\section{What Software Engineering Is}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the main phases of the software lifecycle;
  \item identify common reasons why software projects fail;
  \item explain the role of professional practice in software engineering;
  \item understand the purpose of software quality assurance;
  \item recognise key terms used in the discipline.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Software lifecycle}

The \term{software lifecycle} is a structured sequence of activities that takes a software idea from concept to retirement. The most widely taught model is the \term{waterfall} model, which divides work into distinct phases:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Requirements gathering.} Stakeholders state what the system must do. The output is a requirements specification.
  \item \term{Design.} Architects decide how the system will be built, choosing components, interfaces and data structures.
  \item \term{Implementation.} Developers write source code that follows the design.
  \item \term{Testing.} Testers verify that the code meets the requirements and that defects are removed.
  \item \term{Deployment.} The system is installed in its target environment and made available to users.
  \item \term{Maintenance.} Bugs are fixed, and new features are added as the environment changes.
\end{itemize}

Modern practice often uses iterative models such as \term{Agile}. In an Agile sprint, a small set of requirements is selected, designed, built, tested and delivered in a short cycle, typically two to four weeks. The cycle then repeats, allowing the product to evolve based on feedback.

\begin{example}
A small startup adopts Scrum, an Agile framework. In the first sprint the team delivers a login screen and basic user profile page. After a demo, the product owner requests a password reset feature. The next sprint incorporates this change, and the product grows incrementally rather than waiting for a single, large release.
\end{example}

\subsection{Why projects fail}

Despite careful planning, many software projects do not achieve their goals. Research identifies several recurring causes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Unclear requirements.} When stakeholders cannot agree on what the system should do, developers build the wrong functionality.
  \item \term{Scope creep.} New features are added without adjusting time or resources, leading to overruns.
  \item \term{Poor estimation.} Under estimating effort or complexity results in missed deadlines and budget deficits.
  \item \term{Inadequate testing.} Defects escape to production, causing downtime, user dissatisfaction and costly rework.
  \item \term{Lack of communication.} Misunderstandings between developers, testers, managers and customers create delays and rework.
  \item \term{Technical debt.} Short term shortcuts accumulate, making later changes harder and slower.
\end{itemize}

Addressing these factors requires disciplined processes, realistic planning and continuous stakeholder involvement.

\begin{example}
A government agency commissions a new records system. The initial contract specifies only high level goals. As development proceeds, each department adds its own custom reports. The project's budget doubles, the deadline slips by twelve months, and the final product is rejected because it does not meet the original regulatory requirements.
\end{example}

\subsection{Professional practice}

Software engineering is not only a set of technical activities; it is also a profession with ethical and legal responsibilities. Professional practice includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Code of ethics.} Engineers pledge to protect public safety, maintain confidentiality, and avoid conflicts of interest. The ACM/IEEE Code of Ethics is a common reference.
  \item \term{Documentation.} Clear, accurate documentation supports maintenance, knowledge transfer and legal compliance.
  \item \term{Quality standards.} Standards such as ISO/IEC 12207 define processes for software lifecycle management.
  \item \term{Risk management.} Identifying, analysing and mitigating risks helps prevent catastrophic failures.
  \item \term{Continuous learning.} Technology evolves rapidly; professionals must keep skills up to date through training and certification.
\end{itemize}

Professional practice also involves teamwork. Roles such as \term{project manager}, \term{architect}, \term{developer}, \term{tester} and \term{user experience designer} each contribute specific expertise. Effective collaboration relies on shared artefacts (requirements, design models, test plans) and clear communication channels.

\subsection{Software quality assurance}

\term{Software quality assurance} (SQA) is the systematic activity of ensuring that software processes and products meet defined quality criteria. SQA differs from testing in that it looks at the whole development process, not just the final code. Key activities include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Process audits.} Independent reviews check whether the team follows agreed procedures and standards.
  \item \term{Metrics collection.} Data such as defect density or test coverage are gathered to monitor quality trends.
  \item \term{Configuration management.} Controlled handling of code, documents and libraries prevents accidental loss or inconsistency.
  \item \term{Peer reviews.} Team members examine each other's work, catching defects early and sharing knowledge.
\end{itemize}

By integrating SQA, organisations can detect problems early, reduce rework and increase confidence in the delivered product.

\begin{example}
A mid size software house introduces a peer review step before each code commit. Developers pair up to examine new modules, checking for coding standards compliance and logical errors. Over three months the number of defects reported in later testing drops by twenty percent, and the team delivers releases on schedule more consistently.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Imagine you are leading a new mobile app project. List the lifecycle phases you would schedule, name two common failure causes you must guard against, and cite one ethical principle that will guide your team's decisions.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Software lifecycle & Ordered set of activities from idea to retirement of a software system. \\
Requirements gathering & Process of eliciting and documenting what the system must do. \\
Agile & Iterative development approach that delivers small increments frequently. \\
Scope creep & Uncontrolled expansion of project scope without corresponding resources. \\
Technical debt & Accumulated shortcuts that increase future maintenance cost. \\
Code of ethics & Professional standards that define responsible behaviour for engineers. \\
Risk management & Systematic identification and mitigation of potential project problems. \\
Software quality assurance & Activities that ensure processes and products meet defined quality criteria. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Requirements}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe why gathering requirements is the first step in system development;
  \item differentiate between functional and non functional requirements;
  \item list common techniques for eliciting requirements;
  \item explain how requirements are prioritised and documented;
  \item recognise examples of functional and non functional requirements in a simple scenario;
  \item understand how requirements are validated before design begins;
  \item identify additional considerations for managing requirements throughout a project;
  \item appreciate the role of stakeholder analysis in shaping the requirements set.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Why gather requirements?}

Before any code is written, the development team must understand what the system is supposed to do. This understanding is captured as \term{requirements}. Requirements act as a contract between the client (the person or organisation that wants the system) and the developers. If the contract is vague, the final product may miss important features, cost more time, or fail to satisfy users.

Gathering requirements reduces risk. Early clarification prevents later rework, which is usually far more expensive than the time spent on careful analysis. It also provides a basis for estimating effort, planning milestones, and testing later on.

\subsection{Techniques for eliciting requirements}

Several techniques help analysts collect requirements from stakeholders:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Interviews}. One to one or group conversations where the analyst asks open ended questions about goals, tasks, and constraints.
  \item \term{Questionnaires}. Written surveys that reach many users quickly, useful for gathering quantitative preferences.
  \item \term{Workshops}. Structured sessions where stakeholders brainstorm, prioritise, and negotiate requirements together.
  \item \term{Observation}. Watching users perform their current tasks to discover implicit needs and hidden problems.
  \item \term{Document analysis}. Reviewing existing manuals, reports, and regulations that may impose requirements on the new system.
\end{itemize}

Each technique has strengths. Interviews provide depth, questionnaires provide breadth, workshops encourage consensus, observation reveals real behaviour, and document analysis uncovers legal or legacy constraints. A good requirements effort mixes several methods to obtain a balanced view.

\subsection{Functional requirements}

\term{Functional requirements} describe what the system must do. They are expressed as actions, inputs, outputs, or behaviours. Typical phrasing uses verbs such as ``shall'', ``must'', or ``will''. Examples include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The system shall allow a registered student to enrol in a course.
  \item The system shall calculate the total tuition fee based on selected courses and applicable discounts.
  \item The system shall generate a PDF receipt after a successful payment.
\end{itemize}

Functional requirements are often captured in use cases or user stories. A simple user story format is: ``As a role, I want feature so that benefit.'' This keeps the focus on the user's goal and the value delivered.

\begin{example}
A library management system has the functional requirement: ``The system shall enable a librarian to add a new book record by entering title, author, ISBN, and shelf location.'' This requirement tells exactly what data must be captured and who performs the action.
\end{example}

\subsection{Non functional requirements}

\term{Non functional requirements} (NFRs) describe how the system performs its functions. They are quality attributes, constraints, or standards that the system must satisfy. Common categories include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Performance}. Response time, throughput, or latency limits. Example: ``The system shall return search results within two seconds for ninety five percent of queries.''
  \item \term{Reliability}. Availability, mean time between failures, or error handling. Example: ``The system shall be available ninety nine point nine percent of the time each month.''
  \item \term{Security}. Authentication, authorization, encryption, and audit trails. Example: ``All passwords shall be stored using a salted SHA 256 hash.''
  \item \term{Usability}. Ease of learning, accessibility, or user interface standards. Example: ``New users shall be able to complete the registration process in less than five minutes without assistance.''
  \item \term{Maintainability}. Modularity, documentation, or coding standards. Example: ``The codebase shall achieve at least eighty percent statement coverage by automated unit tests.''
  \item \term{Scalability}. Ability to handle growth in users or data volume. Example: ``The system shall support up to ten thousand concurrent users without degradation of response time.''
\end{itemize}

Non functional requirements are often harder to verify because they involve measurement or subjective judgement. Clear, testable criteria are essential to avoid ambiguity.

\begin{example}
In an online exam platform, a non functional requirement might state: ``The system shall encrypt all data transmitted between the client browser and the server using TLS 1.2 or higher.'' This requirement does not describe a user action, but it sets a security standard that must be met.
\end{example}

\subsection{Prioritising and documenting requirements}

Not all requirements have equal importance. Prioritisation helps the team decide which items must be delivered first, which can be deferred, and which are optional. A common approach is the MoSCoW method, which classifies requirements as Must have, Should have, Could have, and Won\textquotesingle t have this time. This classification guides planning and negotiation with the client.

Documentation should reflect both classification and traceability. Each requirement receives a unique identifier, such as FR01 for a functional requirement or NFR03 for a non functional requirement. The document typically contains:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Scope}. What is in scope and out of scope for the project.
  \item \term{Assumptions}. Conditions taken as true for the purpose of analysis.
  \item \term{Constraints}. Technical, regulatory, or budgetary limits.
  \item \term{Glossary}. Definitions of domain specific terms.
  \item \term{Priority}. The MoSCoW label or another ranking.
\end{itemize}

A traceability matrix links each requirement identifier to design elements, implementation modules, and test cases. This matrix ensures that every requirement is addressed throughout the development lifecycle and that no high priority item is omitted.

\begin{example}
For a campus parking app, the team might assign FR02 the label Must have and write: ``The system shall allow a driver to reserve a parking spot for a selected date and time.'' The same matrix would show that this requirement is linked to the reservation module, the database schema for bookings, and the acceptance test that verifies successful reservation.
\end{example}

\subsection{Validating requirements}

After requirements have been gathered and documented, they must be checked for correctness, completeness, and feasibility. Validation involves reviewing the requirements with stakeholders to ensure they reflect the true needs of the users and the constraints of the environment.

Common validation activities include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Peer reviews}. Team members examine each requirement for clarity, consistency, and testability.
  \item \term{Prototyping}. Building a quick mockup of a user interface or a critical function to confirm that the requirement makes sense in practice.
  \item \term{Walkthroughs}. Structured meetings where the analyst walks stakeholders through the requirement document and records feedback.
  \item \term{Acceptance criteria definition}. Adding precise conditions that must be met for a requirement to be considered satisfied.
\end{itemize}

Validation helps catch ambiguous wording, missing dependencies, or unrealistic expectations before costly design and coding begin. It also builds stakeholder confidence that their concerns are being heard and addressed.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Given a brief description of a new campus parking app, list two functional requirements and two non functional requirements that you would expect to see in the requirements document.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Managing requirements throughout the project}

Requirements are not static; they evolve as the project progresses and as new information emerges. Effective management ensures that changes are controlled, communicated, and reflected in all artefacts.

Key practices include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Change control}. A formal process for submitting, reviewing, and approving modifications to existing requirements.
  \item \term{Versioning}. Assigning a version number to the requirements document and maintaining a history of revisions.
  \item \term{Impact analysis}. Assessing how a proposed change affects scope, schedule, cost, and downstream artefacts such as design and test cases.
  \item \term{Stakeholder communication}. Regular updates to all parties about approved changes and the current status of requirements.
\end{itemize}

By treating requirements as living artefacts, teams can adapt to shifting business needs while preserving traceability and avoiding scope creep.

\subsection{Stakeholder analysis}

Understanding who the stakeholders are and what they need is a prerequisite for effective requirements work. \term{Stakeholder analysis} identifies all parties who have an interest in the system, classifies them by influence and interest, and records their specific concerns.

The analysis typically produces a simple list that includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Name or role of the stakeholder;
  \item Primary goals or problems the stakeholder wants the system to address;
  \item Level of influence on project decisions;
  \item Preferred communication channel.
\end{itemize}

Having this information helps the analyst choose the most appropriate elicitation technique for each group, anticipate conflicts, and ensure that high impact stakeholders are consulted early. It also provides a basis for prioritising requirements that align with the most critical business objectives.

\begin{example}
In a student information system project, the stakeholder analysis might list: ``Registrar -- needs accurate enrolment data, high influence, prefers formal meetings; Students -- need easy course selection, medium influence, prefers online surveys; IT security officer -- needs compliance with data protection policies, high influence, prefers document reviews.'' This analysis guides the selection of interviews with the registrar, questionnaires for students, and document analysis for security policies.
\end{example}

\subsection{Specification languages for requirements}

A requirement can be written in free text, but many projects adopt a lightweight specification language to improve clarity and testability. One common approach is to use a structured template that separates the condition, the action, and the expected result.

The template typically looks like:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Given}. The context or pre condition.
  \item \term{When}. The event or trigger.
  \item \term{Then}. The observable outcome.
\end{itemize}

Using this format forces the writer to state the exact circumstance under which the requirement applies and the measurable effect that must occur. It also aligns well with automated acceptance testing tools.

\begin{example}
For a mobile banking app, a functional requirement expressed in the template could be: ``Given a logged in user, when the user selects the transfer funds option and enters a valid recipient and amount, then the system shall debit the source account, credit the destination account, and display a confirmation message.'' This version removes ambiguity about the required state before the action and the observable result after the action.
\end{example}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify three techniques you could use to gather requirements for a student council voting system. Explain one advantage of each technique.
  \item Write a functional requirement for a system that allows a professor to upload lecture slides. Use the ``shall'' phrasing.
  \item Write a non functional requirement that addresses the security of the same lecture upload feature.
  \item Explain why it is important to assign unique identifiers to each requirement.
  \item Using the MoSCoW method, classify the following requirement: ``The system shall display a weather widget on the dashboard.'' Indicate whether it is a Must have, Should have, Could have, or Won\textquotesingle t have this time.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Requirements & Statements that describe what a system must do or how it must behave. \\
Functional requirement & A requirement that specifies a particular function, action, or behaviour of the system. \\
Non functional requirement & A requirement that specifies quality attributes, constraints, or standards for the system. \\
Elicitation & The process of gathering requirements from stakeholders using techniques such as interviews or observation. \\
Traceability & The ability to link each requirement to design elements, implementation, and test cases. \\
Use case & A description of how a user interacts with the system to achieve a goal, often used to capture functional requirements. \\
MoSCoW & A prioritisation technique that classifies requirements as Must have, Should have, Could have, or Won\textquotesingle t have this time. \\
Validation & Activities that check requirements for correctness, completeness, and feasibility before design begins. \\
Change control & A formal process for managing modifications to requirements throughout a project. \\
Stakeholder analysis & The identification and classification of people or groups with an interest in the system, used to guide elicitation and prioritisation. \\
Specification template & A structured format using Given, When, Then to make requirements clear and testable. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Software Design}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{modularity} and explain why it matters in large programs;
  \item distinguish between \term{coupling} and \term{cohesion} and assess a design on these criteria;
  \item recognise common \term{design patterns} and describe the problem each pattern solves;
  \item apply a simple design pattern to improve an existing piece of code.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Modularity}

\term{Modularity} means dividing a software system into separate parts, called modules, that can be developed, understood and tested independently. Each module should have a clear purpose and a well defined interface. The interface lists the services the module offers and the inputs it requires, while hiding the internal implementation.

A modular system has several practical benefits:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Manageability.} A programmer can focus on one module without being overwhelmed by the whole code base.
  \item \textbf{Reusability.} A module that solves a generic problem (for example, a logging component) can be reused in many projects.
  \item \textbf{Replaceability.} If a module becomes obsolete, it can be swapped for a new one as long as the interface stays the same.
  \item \textbf{Parallel development.} Different team members can work on different modules at the same time, reducing schedule risk.
\end{itemize}

A common way to achieve modularity is to group related functions, classes or data structures into a package or namespace. In object oriented languages, a class often represents a module, and the public methods form the interface.

\begin{example}
Consider a simple e commerce application. The payment processing code is placed in a module called \texttt{PaymentGateway}. All other parts of the system call the single function \texttt{processPayment(amount, cardInfo)}. If the business later decides to switch from a credit card processor to a digital wallet service, only the \texttt{PaymentGateway} module needs to be changed; the rest of the application continues to work unchanged.
\end{example}

\subsection{Coupling and Cohesion}

\term{Coupling} describes how strongly one module depends on another. Low (or loose) coupling means a module uses only the minimal information it needs from other modules. High (or tight) coupling means a module knows many details about another module's internal workings. Loose coupling is desirable because it reduces the ripple effect of changes.

\term{Cohesion} measures how closely the responsibilities inside a single module are related. High cohesion means the module does one thing and does it well. Low cohesion means the module mixes unrelated responsibilities, making it harder to understand and maintain.

The two concepts are often evaluated together:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{High cohesion + low coupling} is the ideal. Each module has a single, clear purpose and interacts with others through simple interfaces.
  \item \textbf{Low cohesion + high coupling} leads to tangled code that is difficult to test and modify.
\end{itemize}

Typical coupling types, from strongest to weakest, are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Content coupling.} One module directly modifies the internal data of another.
  \item \textbf{Common data coupling.} Modules share global variables.
  \item \textbf{Control coupling.} One module passes a control flag that influences the internal logic of another.
  \item \textbf{Stamp coupling.} Modules exchange complex data structures but use only part of them.
  \item \textbf{Data coupling.} Modules communicate only through simple parameters (e.g., numbers or strings).
\end{itemize}

Cohesion types, from weakest to strongest, include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Coincidental cohesion.} Unrelated functions are placed together.
  \item \textbf{Logical cohesion.} Functions are grouped because they are logically related, but the module performs several distinct tasks.
  \item \textbf{Functional cohesion.} All parts of the module contribute to a single well defined task.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A class \texttt{UserManager} that handles user authentication, email notification, and file system cleanup exhibits low cohesion: authentication, messaging and cleanup are unrelated concerns. Moreover, if other classes directly read the private fields of \texttt{UserManager}, the system suffers from high coupling. Refactoring into three classes---\texttt{Authenticator}, \texttt{Notifier}, and \texttt{Cleaner}---improves both cohesion and coupling.
\end{example}

\subsection{Design Patterns}

\term{Design patterns} are reusable solutions to common design problems. They capture proven ideas in a language independent form, allowing developers to communicate and apply best practices without reinventing the wheel. Patterns do not provide ready made code; they describe the structure of classes and objects and the collaborations that solve a particular problem.

The most widely used catalog is the \term{Gang of Four} (GoF) collection, which groups patterns into three categories:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Creational patterns.} Deal with object creation mechanisms. Examples: \term{Singleton}, \term{Factory Method}, \term{Abstract Factory}, \term{Builder}, \term{Prototype}.
  \item \textbf{Structural patterns.} Concern how classes and objects are composed to form larger structures. Examples: \term{Adapter}, \term{Decorator}, \term{Facade}, \term{Composite}, \term{Proxy}, \term{Bridge}, \term{Flyweight}.
  \item \textbf{Behavioral patterns.} Focus on communication between objects. Examples: \term{Observer}, \term{Strategy}, \term{Command}, \term{Iterator}, \term{Mediator}, \term{State}, \term{Visitor}, \term{Template Method}.
\end{itemize}

Each pattern typically includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Intent.} The goal the pattern solves.
  \item \textbf{Motivation.} A concrete scenario that illustrates the problem.
  \item \textbf{Structure.} Class diagram or relationships.
  \item \textbf{Consequences.} Benefits and trade offs.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
The \term{Observer} pattern solves the problem of keeping several objects in sync with a subject's state. In a graphical user interface, a data model (the subject) can notify multiple view components (observers) whenever the data changes. The model maintains a list of observers and calls a predefined update method on each. This decouples the model from the views: the model knows only that observers implement an interface, not what concrete view classes exist.
\end{example}

\subsection{Applying a Simple Pattern: Factory Method}

The \term{Factory Method} pattern provides an interface for creating objects, but lets subclasses decide which concrete class to instantiate. It is useful when a system must be independent of how its objects are created, composed or represented.

Suppose a reporting tool must generate output in either PDF or HTML format. Without a pattern, client code would contain conditional statements such as:

\[
\text{if (type == "PDF") createPdfReport(); else createHtmlReport();}
\]

This ties the client to concrete classes and makes adding a new format difficult. Using a Factory Method, we define an abstract creator \texttt{ReportFactory} with a method \texttt{createReport()}. Subclasses \texttt{PdfReportFactory} and \texttt{HtmlReportFactory} override the method to return the appropriate concrete report object. The client now works only with the abstract factory and the abstract \texttt{Report} interface.

Benefits include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Open/Closed principle.} New report types can be added by creating a new factory subclass without modifying existing code.
  \item \textbf{Encapsulation of creation logic.} Object construction details are hidden inside the factory.
  \item \textbf{Improved testability.} Factories can be mocked to supply test doubles.
\end{itemize}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When you encounter a piece of code that decides which class to instantiate based on a parameter or configuration, ask: ``Would moving the decision into a separate factory class reduce the number of conditionals in the client?'' If the answer is yes, the Factory Method pattern is a suitable refactoring.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Modularity & Division of a system into independent, interchangeable components with clear interfaces. \\
Coupling & Degree of interdependence between modules; lower coupling is preferred. \\
Cohesion & Degree to which the elements inside a module belong together; higher cohesion is preferred. \\
Design pattern & A reusable, language independent solution to a common software design problem. \\
Singleton & Creational pattern that ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global access point. \\
Factory Method & Creational pattern that defines an interface for creating objects, letting subclasses decide which class to instantiate. \\
Observer & Behavioral pattern that defines a one to many dependency so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified. \\
Adapter & Structural pattern that allows incompatible interfaces to work together by converting one interface into another. \\
Facade & Structural pattern that provides a simplified interface to a complex subsystem. \\
Strategy & Behavioral pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{Development Methods}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the main phases of the waterfall model and its typical use cases;
  \item contrast waterfall with iterative and agile approaches;
  \item explain what a sprint is and how it fits into agile development;
  \item understand the basic idea of a Kanban workflow and when it is appropriate;
  \item choose an appropriate development method for a given project scenario.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Waterfall model}

The \term{waterfall model} is a linear sequence of phases. Each phase must be completed before the next one begins. The classic phases are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Requirements -- gather what the system must do;
  \item Design -- plan the architecture and components;
  \item Implementation -- write the code;
  \item Verification -- test the code against the requirements;
  \item Maintenance -- fix bugs and add minor enhancements after release.
\end{itemize}

Because the model is linear, changes after a phase is finished are expensive. Documentation is produced at each stage and handed to the next team. This makes waterfall attractive for projects with well defined, stable requirements, such as regulatory systems or hardware driven products.

\begin{example}
A government agency commissions a tax calculation system. The legal rules are fixed for several years. The project team writes a detailed requirements specification, gets formal approval, then proceeds through design, coding, and testing in strict order. Any change to the tax law after the design phase would require revisiting the earlier documents, increasing cost and delay.
\end{example}

\subsection{Iterative and agile approaches}

Iterative development breaks the work into repeated cycles, called \term{iterations}. Each iteration produces a small, usable piece of the system. Feedback from the previous iteration is used to improve the next one. The key ideas are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Build a minimal version first;
  \item Test and evaluate early;
  \item Refine the product in successive cycles.
\end{itemize}

\term{Agile} is a family of iterative methods that adds a set of values and principles, such as customer collaboration, responding to change, and delivering working software frequently. Popular agile frameworks include Scrum, Kanban and Extreme Programming. Agile teams work in short time boxes, keep communication open, and adapt plans as new information appears.

Compared with waterfall, agile methods:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Accept changing requirements throughout the project;
  \item Emphasise face to face communication over heavy documentation;
  \item Deliver functional increments every few weeks;
  \item Encourage self organising teams.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A start up builds a mobile app for social networking. Market trends shift quickly, so the product owner continuously reprioritises features. The development team works in two week cycles, delivering a prototype at the end of each cycle, gathering user feedback, and adjusting the backlog before the next cycle.
\end{example}

\subsection{Sprints}

A \term{sprint} is a fixed length iteration used in Scrum, typically one to four weeks long. At the start of a sprint the team holds a \term{Sprint Planning} meeting to select items from the product backlog. The selected items form the \term{Sprint Backlog}. During the sprint the team:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Develops, tests and integrates the chosen features;
  \item Holds a daily \term{stand up} to coordinate work;
  \item Avoids adding new work that is not in the sprint backlog.
\end{itemize}

At the end of the sprint a \term{Sprint Review} demonstrates the increment to stakeholders, and a \term{Sprint Retrospective} reflects on the process to identify improvements. The regular cadence of sprints creates a predictable rhythm and makes progress visible.

\subsection{Kanban workflow}

\term{Kanban} is a visual workflow management method that originated in manufacturing and is now used in software development. Work items are represented by cards on a board divided into columns such as \term{To Do}, \term{In Progress} and \term{Done}. The board shows the current state of every task at a glance.

Key practices of Kanban include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Limiting work in progress -- only a set number of items may be in the \term{In Progress} column at any time;
  \item Managing flow -- the team monitors how quickly cards move from left to right and looks for bottlenecks;
  \item Continuous delivery -- there is no fixed iteration length; features are released when they are ready.
\end{itemize}

Kanban is useful when a team needs flexibility, when work arrives unpredictably, or when the focus is on improving throughput rather than delivering fixed time boxed increments.

\begin{example}
An operations support team handles incoming service requests. Each request is entered as a card on a Kanban board. The team limits the number of cards in the \term{In Progress} column to three, ensuring that members finish existing work before starting new tasks. Over several weeks the team observes that most delays occur at the testing stage, prompting them to add a dedicated testing column and allocate additional resources there.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When a project has stable, well documented requirements and a fixed deadline, waterfall is often suitable. When requirements are expected to evolve, when early user feedback is valuable, or when the team needs flexibility, an iterative or agile method---especially one using sprints---is usually a better fit. For environments with continuous incoming work and a need to optimise flow, a Kanban workflow provides a lightweight alternative to time boxed iterations.
\end{keytest}

