\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Version Control}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe what a commit records and how to create one;
  \item explain the purpose of branches and how they isolate work;
  \item outline the steps required to merge changes from one branch to another;
  \item compare common collaboration workflows such as centralized, feature branch, and fork and pull;
  \item define what a tag is, why it is useful, and how to create and use tags in a repository.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Commits}

A \term{commit} is a snapshot of the project files at a particular moment. When you commit, the version control system stores three pieces of information:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the exact content of every tracked file;
  \item a short message that explains why the change was made;
  \item metadata such as the author's name, email and the time of the commit.
\end{itemize}

Each commit receives a unique identifier. This identifier allows you to refer to the exact state of the project later, for example to revert a mistake or to compare two versions. Good commit practice includes making small, focused changes and writing clear messages. A typical message has a concise headline followed by an optional longer description that explains the reasoning behind the change.

\begin{example}
You edit \texttt{analysis.py} to fix a division by zero error. After testing, you run:

\texttt{git add analysis.py} \\
\texttt{git commit -m "Fix division by zero in compute\_average"}

The system records a new commit containing the corrected file, the message, and your author information. Later you can view this commit with \texttt{git log} and see exactly when and why the fix was introduced.
\end{example}

\subsection{Branches}

A \term{branch} is a lightweight pointer to a commit. The default branch in most repositories is called \texttt{main}. Creating a new branch gives you an independent line of development that starts from the current commit but can diverge without affecting other branches. Because branches share history, they use very little extra storage; only the differences between commits are recorded.

Branches are useful for several reasons:

\begin{itemize}
  \item developing a new feature while the main codebase stays stable;
  \item experimenting with an alternative algorithm without risking the main line;
  \item isolating a bug fix so it can be reviewed before being added to the main line.
\end{itemize}

Switching between branches changes the working directory to reflect the files stored in the target commit. When you finish work on a branch, you can merge it back into the main line or discard it if the experiment was not successful.

\subsection{Merging}

\term{Merging} combines the histories of two branches. The most common case is to bring a feature branch back into the main line after the work is complete. Git performs a three way merge using:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the common ancestor commit of the two branches;
  \item the tip of the current branch;
  \item the tip of the branch being merged.
\end{itemize}

If the same part of a file was changed in both branches, Git reports a \term{conflict}. Conflicts must be resolved manually: you edit the affected files to keep the desired changes, then mark the conflict as resolved and create a new merge commit.

When the current branch has no new commits since the branch diverged, Git can perform a fast forward merge. In that case the branch pointer is simply moved forward and no extra merge commit is created.

\begin{example}
After finishing the PDF export feature, you return to \texttt{main} and run:

\texttt{git merge pdf-export}

Git sees that \texttt{main} has not changed since the branch was created, so it fast forwards \texttt{main} to the tip of \texttt{pdf-export}. The history now shows a single line with the new feature added.
\end{example}

\subsection{Collaboration Workflows}

Different teams adopt different workflows to balance safety, speed and simplicity. The three most common patterns are described below.

\subsubsection{Centralized workflow}

All developers push directly to the shared \texttt{main} branch. Before pushing, each developer pulls the latest changes, resolves any conflicts locally, and then pushes. This workflow is easy to understand but can lead to unstable main branches if many people push untested code.

\subsubsection{Feature branch workflow}

Developers create a short lived branch for each task. Work is committed locally, then the branch is pushed to the central server. A pull request (or merge request) is opened, allowing teammates to review the changes. After approval, the branch is merged into \texttt{main}. The main branch remains stable because only reviewed code is integrated.

\subsubsection{Fork and pull workflow}

Common in open source projects, each contributor forks the main repository into their own account. They work on a personal clone, push commits to their fork, and then open a pull request against the upstream repository. Maintainers review and merge the pull request. This model isolates external contributors from the core repository and provides a clear audit trail.

All three workflows rely on the same underlying concepts---commits, branches and merges---but differ in who creates branches, who reviews code, and how often the main line is updated.

\subsection{Tags}

A \term{tag} is a human readable label that points to a specific commit. Tags are often used to mark important points in a project's history, such as releases or milestones. Unlike branches, tags do not move; they remain fixed on the commit they were created on. Tags can be lightweight (just a name) or annotated (including a message, author and date).

Tags are useful for several reasons:

\begin{itemize}
  \item they provide an easy way to refer to a released version;
  \item they allow tools to automatically generate release notes based on the commit range between tags;
  \item they give users a stable reference for downloading source code archives.
\end{itemize}

Creating a tag is straightforward. After committing the code that you want to mark, you run a command that creates the tag and optionally pushes it to the remote repository.

\begin{example}
You have just finished version 1.0 of a library. The latest commit hash is abc123. To create an annotated tag you run:

\texttt{git tag -a v1.0 -m "First stable release"} \\
\texttt{git push origin v1.0}

The tag \texttt{v1.0} now points to commit abc123. Anyone can check out the exact code for version 1.0 with \texttt{git checkout v1.0}.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Choose a scenario: a small class project with two members, a medium size research group with ten members, or a public open source library with many external contributors. Which workflow (centralized, feature branch, or fork and pull) best matches the need for stability, review and contributor isolation? Explain your choice in one sentence.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Commit & A recorded snapshot of the project with a message, author and timestamp. \\
Branch & A movable pointer to a commit that creates an independent line of development. \\
Merge & The operation that combines two branch histories, possibly creating a new commit. \\
Conflict & A situation where the same part of a file was edited differently in two branches, requiring manual resolution. \\
Feature branch workflow & A collaborative pattern where each task is developed on its own branch and merged after review. \\
Fork and pull workflow & An open source pattern where contributors work in personal copies and submit pull requests to the upstream repository. \\
Tag & A fixed label that points to a specific commit, often used to mark releases or milestones. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{Testing}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{unit testing} and explain why it is useful;
  \item describe \term{integration testing} and how it differs from unit testing;
  \item explain the concept of \term{test coverage} and list common coverage metrics;
  \item outline the purpose of a \term{bug tracking} system and identify key elements of a good bug report;
  \item understand the role of \term{test automation} in a modern development workflow and create a simple automated test script;
  \item summarise the principles of \term{test driven development} and its impact on software design;
  \item describe the function of \term{continuous integration} and how it supports rapid feedback.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Unit testing}

\term{Unit testing} is the practice of checking the smallest testable parts of a program---usually individual functions or methods---in isolation from the rest of the system. A unit test supplies a set of inputs, runs the code under test, and compares the actual output with the expected result. If the two match, the test passes; otherwise it fails.

The main benefits of unit testing are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Early detection of defects.} Errors are caught as soon as the code is written, before they propagate to other parts of the program.
  \item \textbf{Documentation of intent.} Each test shows how the developer expects the unit to behave for a particular scenario.
  \item \textbf{Facilitates refactoring.} When the code is changed, the existing test suite quickly reveals whether the change broke any behaviour.
  \item \textbf{Supports automation.} Unit tests can be run automatically on every code commit, giving continuous feedback.
\end{itemize}

A good unit test is small, deterministic, and independent of external resources such as databases, files, or network services. When a unit depends on such resources, developers often replace them with \term{test doubles} (mocks, stubs, or fakes) that simulate the real behaviour in a controlled way.

\begin{example}
Consider a function \texttt{addTax(price, rate)} that returns the price plus tax. A unit test might look like:

\begin{verbatim}
assert addTax(100, 0.05) == 105
assert addTax(0, 0.10) == 0
assert addTax(200, 0) == 200
\end{verbatim}

Each call supplies concrete arguments and checks the exact numeric result. The test does not need a database, a user interface, or any other part of the application.
\end{example}

\subsection{Integration testing}

\term{Integration testing} checks how multiple units work together. After individual units have passed their own tests, they are combined into larger subsystems, and the interactions between them are exercised. The goal is to uncover defects that arise only when components are linked, such as mismatched data formats, incorrect API usage, or unexpected side effects.

Key characteristics of integration testing include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Broader scope.} Tests cover more than one module, often an entire layer of the architecture.
  \item \textbf{Real dependencies.} Unlike unit tests, integration tests usually involve real databases, file systems, or network services, because the purpose is to verify that those connections work correctly.
  \item \textbf{Fewer tests, higher cost.} Each test takes longer to run and may require more setup, so the number of integration tests is typically smaller than the number of unit tests.
  \item \textbf{Detects interface problems.} Errors such as a function returning a different data type than its caller expects are revealed.
\end{itemize}

Integration testing can be performed in several ways. A \term{bottom up} approach starts with low level components and gradually adds higher layers, while a \term{top down} approach begins with the user interface layer and replaces lower layers with stubs. A \term{big bang} approach assembles the whole system at once, but it often makes debugging harder because many failures appear together.

\begin{example}
A web application has a \texttt{UserService} that calls a \texttt{UserRepository} to store user data in a SQL database. An integration test might:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start a test database (or use an in memory database);
  \item Call \texttt{UserService.createUser("Alice", "alice@example.com")};
  \item Query the database directly to verify that a row with the correct name and email exists;
  \item Clean up by deleting the test data.
\end{enumerate}

The test checks that the service correctly builds the SQL statement, that the repository executes it, and that the database stores the data as expected.
\end{example}

\subsection{Test coverage}

\term{Test coverage} measures how much of a program's source code is exercised by the test suite. Coverage is expressed as a percentage; higher percentages indicate that more code paths have been run during testing. Coverage does not guarantee correctness, but it helps identify untested areas that might hide bugs.

Common coverage metrics are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Statement coverage.} The proportion of executable statements that have been executed at least once.
  \item \textbf{Branch coverage.} The proportion of decision points where each possible branch has been taken.
  \item \textbf{Condition coverage.} The proportion of Boolean sub expressions that have been evaluated to both true and false.
  \item \textbf{Path coverage.} The proportion of distinct execution paths through the code. Full path coverage is usually infeasible for large programs.
\end{itemize}

Tools such as \texttt{coverage.py} for Python, \texttt{JaCoCo} for Java, or \texttt{gcov} for C generate coverage reports after a test run. A typical workflow is:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Run the full test suite with the coverage tool enabled;
  \item Examine the report to locate lines or branches that were never executed;
  \item Write additional tests to cover those gaps;
  \item Repeat until the desired coverage threshold (often eighty percent or higher) is reached.
\end{enumerate}

It is important to remember that one hundred percent coverage does not mean the software is bug free. Tests may still miss logical errors, incorrect assumptions, or performance problems. Coverage should be used as a guide, not as an absolute quality metric.

\subsection{Bug tracking}

\term{Bug tracking} is the systematic recording, prioritising, and managing of defects discovered during development or after release. A bug tracker provides a shared repository where developers, testers, and stakeholders can view the status of each issue and collaborate on its resolution.

Essential elements of a good bug report include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Title.} A concise summary that captures the core problem.
  \item \textbf{Description.} A clear narrative of what was expected, what actually happened, and why the behaviour is considered a defect.
  \item \textbf{Steps to reproduce.} Ordered actions that reliably trigger the bug, including input data, configuration settings, and environment details.
  \item \textbf{Severity and priority.} An assessment of how much the bug impacts users (severity) and how urgently it should be fixed (priority).
  \item \textbf{Attachments.} Screenshots, log files, or stack traces that provide additional evidence.
  \item \textbf{Assignee and status.} The person responsible for fixing the bug and the current workflow stage (open, in progress, resolved, closed).
\end{itemize}

Effective bug tracking promotes transparency and accountability. It also enables metrics such as mean time to resolve and defect density, which help teams assess their quality processes.

Modern bug trackers (for example, Jira, GitHub Issues, Redmine) often integrate with version control systems. When a developer references a bug identifier in a commit message, the system can automatically update the bug's status, linking code changes to the reported problem.

\subsection{Continuous integration}

\term{Continuous integration} (CI) is a practice where developers merge their changes into a shared repository frequently, often several times a day. Each merge triggers an automated build that compiles the code, runs the full test suite, and reports the results. CI provides rapid feedback about the health of the codebase, allowing teams to detect integration problems early.

Key elements of a CI pipeline include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Automated build.} The source code is compiled or interpreted in a clean environment.
  \item \textbf{Automated testing.} All unit, integration, and functional tests are executed automatically.
  \item \textbf{Static analysis.} Tools check code style, security issues, and potential bugs without running the program.
  \item \textbf{Reporting.} The pipeline publishes a clear status indicator (pass or fail) and details of any failures.
\end{itemize}

By enforcing that every change passes the same set of checks, CI reduces the risk of broken builds, encourages small incremental changes, and supports a culture of shared responsibility for quality.

\subsection{Test automation}

\term{Test automation} refers to the use of software tools to execute tests, compare actual outcomes with expected results, and report findings without human intervention. Automation makes it feasible to run large test suites frequently, such as on every code commit or nightly build.

Key aspects of test automation include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Repeatability.} Automated tests run the same way each time, eliminating manual variability.
  \item \textbf{Speed.} Machines can execute thousands of test cases in minutes, far faster than a human could.
  \item \textbf{Integration with build pipelines.} Automated tests are often triggered by continuous integration servers, providing immediate feedback on code quality.
  \item \textbf{Maintainability.} Test scripts should be clear, modular, and easy to update as the application evolves.
\end{itemize}

A simple automated test script can be written in a language such as Python using a testing framework. The script defines test functions, uses assertions to verify behaviour, and can be run from the command line.

\begin{example}
The following Python script uses the \texttt{unittest} framework to automate tests for the previously shown \texttt{addTax} function:

\begin{verbatim}
import unittest

def addTax(price, rate):
    return price + price * rate

class TestAddTax(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_standard_rate(self):
        self.assertEqual(addTax(100, 0.05), 105)

    def test_zero_price(self):
        self.assertEqual(addTax(0, 0.10), 0)

    def test_zero_rate(self):
        self.assertEqual(addTax(200, 0), 200)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    unittest.main()
\end{verbatim}

Running this script executes all three test cases automatically and reports any failures.
\end{example}

\subsection{Test driven development}

\term{Test driven development} (TDD) is a disciplined approach in which developers write a failing test before any production code is added. The cycle consists of three steps: write a test, make the test pass by writing minimal code, then refactor the code while keeping the test green. This tight feedback loop encourages simple, well specified designs.

Key principles of TDD are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Red, green, refactor.} The test initially fails (red), the developer adds just enough code to make it pass (green), and then improves the internal structure without changing behaviour (refactor).
  \item \textbf{Small increments.} Each cycle addresses a tiny piece of functionality, reducing the risk of large, untested changes.
  \item \textbf{Living documentation.} The test suite serves as up to date documentation of how the system is supposed to behave.
  \item \textbf{Design influence.} Because tests must be written first, developers tend to produce loosely coupled, highly cohesive components that are easy to test.
\end{itemize}

Adopting TDD can increase the overall quality of the codebase, lower debugging time, and provide a safety net for future modifications. However, it requires discipline and may initially slow down development until the team becomes comfortable with the rhythm.

