\lessonlabel{7}
\section{Deployment and Maintenance}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the purpose of a software release and the steps that make up a release process;
  \item explain why monitoring is essential after deployment and list common metrics that are observed;
  \item discuss strategies for handling change over time, including version control, configuration management and rollback plans;
  \item outline the role of documentation in supporting deployment and ongoing maintenance activities;
  \item apply a security focused change process from code commit to post deployment verification.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Releases}

A \term{release} is a packaged version of a software system that is made available to users or to a production environment. Releases are not ad hoc copies of code; they follow a defined process that ensures the software works as expected and can be supported.

Typical steps in a release process are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Build.} Source code is compiled, dependencies are resolved and artefacts such as binaries, containers or archives are created.
  \item \term{Test.} Automated unit, integration and system tests are run against the build artefact. A successful test suite is a prerequisite for moving forward.
  \item \term{Package.} The artefact is bundled with configuration files, documentation and any required runtime scripts. The package is given a unique version identifier, often following semantic versioning.
  \item \term{Deploy.} The package is transferred to the target environment. Deployment may be manual, scripted or performed by a continuous delivery pipeline.
  \item \term{Verify.} After deployment, a set of smoke tests confirms that the system starts correctly and basic functionality is available.
  \item \term{Release notes.} A short document lists new features, bug fixes, known issues and any required post deployment actions.
\end{itemize}

Each step adds a layer of confidence. A well structured release process reduces the risk of introducing defects into production and makes it easier to trace which code is running at any time.

\begin{example}
A web application team uses a Git repository with a main branch for production code. When a new feature is ready, a pull request is merged, triggering a CI pipeline. The pipeline builds a Docker image, runs the full test suite, tags the image as v2.3.0, pushes it to a private registry and finally executes a Kubernetes rollout. After the rollout, a health check script runs a set of API calls; all succeed, so the team publishes release notes that mention the added reporting dashboard and a fixed login bug.
\end{example}

\subsection{Monitoring}

Monitoring is the continuous collection, analysis and visualisation of data about a running system. It provides early warning of problems, helps diagnose incidents and supplies evidence for capacity planning.

Key categories of monitoring data include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Availability.} Metrics such as uptime percentage, heartbeat checks and service level indicator measurements tell whether the system is reachable.
  \item \term{Performance.} Response times, request rates, CPU and memory usage, and database query latency reveal how quickly the system handles load.
  \item \term{Error rates.} Counts of exceptions, failed requests or rejected transactions highlight functional problems.
  \item \term{Business metrics.} Numbers of orders, sign ups or other domain specific events connect technical health to business outcomes.
\end{itemize}

Effective monitoring requires three components:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Instrumentation.} Code and infrastructure emit metrics, logs and traces in a standard format.
  \item \term{Collection.} A monitoring platform gathers the data from all sources and stores it for analysis.
  \item \term{Alerting.} Rules define thresholds; when a metric crosses a threshold, an alert is sent to the on call engineer via email, pager or chat.
\end{itemize}

Monitoring is not a one time activity. Dashboards must be reviewed regularly, alerts tuned to avoid noise, and new metrics added as the system evolves.

\begin{example}
An e commerce site configures its application to emit request latency and error count metrics every minute. A Prometheus server scrapes these metrics and stores them. Grafana dashboards show the average latency over the past hour and a spike in error count during a deployment. An alert rule triggers when error count exceeds a defined threshold, sending a message to the incident response channel. The on call engineer investigates, discovers a misconfigured database connection, fixes it, and the error count returns to normal.
\end{example}

\subsection{Handling Change Over Time}

Software does not remain static after the first deployment. New requirements, security patches and performance improvements all require changes. Managing change safely is the core of maintenance.

The main practices are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Version control.} All source code, configuration files and deployment scripts are stored in a repository. Branching strategies allow multiple lines of work to coexist while keeping a clear history.
  \item \term{Configuration management.} Environment specific settings such as database URLs, API keys and feature flags are kept separate from code and managed with tools like Ansible, Chef or Kubernetes ConfigMaps. This prevents accidental changes to production configuration.
  \item \term{Change review.} Peer review of pull requests ensures that at least one other developer examines the proposed modification, checks for bugs and verifies that tests are updated.
  \item \term{Automated testing.} A comprehensive test suite gives confidence that a change does not break existing functionality. Tests are run on every commit and on every release candidate.
  \item \term{Rollback plan.} For each release a clear procedure exists to revert to the previous stable version. This may involve redeploying an earlier artefact, restoring a database backup or toggling a feature flag.
\end{itemize}

When change is frequent, the team may adopt a \term{continuous delivery} model: every change that passes automated checks is automatically released to production. Even in this model, monitoring and rollback remain essential safeguards.

\subsection{Documentation for Deployment and Maintenance}

Documentation is often overlooked but it is a critical component of a reliable deployment and maintenance process. Good documentation captures the knowledge needed to repeat operations, troubleshoot issues and onboard new team members.

Important documentation artefacts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Runbooks.} Step by step instructions for routine tasks such as deploying a new release, scaling the service or performing a database migration.
  \item \term{Incident post mortems.} Structured reports that record what happened during an outage, the root cause analysis, and actions taken to prevent recurrence.
  \item \term{Configuration inventories.} Lists of all configuration items, their locations, and the intended values for each environment.
  \item \term{Dependency maps.} Diagrams that show how services, databases and external APIs are connected, helping engineers understand impact when a component changes.
\end{itemize}

Keeping documentation up to date requires the same discipline as code. Changes to the system should be accompanied by updates to the relevant runbooks or diagrams. Some teams store documentation in the same version control repository as the code, allowing changes to be reviewed together with code changes.

\begin{example}
A microservice team maintains a runbook in a Markdown file that describes the deployment pipeline. When the pipeline is modified to add a new security scan step, the developer updates the runbook to include the new command, the expected output and the verification checklist. A pull request is opened, the change is reviewed, and the updated runbook is merged together with the pipeline code, ensuring that future deployments follow the correct procedure.
\end{example}

\subsection{Security Patch Management}

Applying security patches is a specialised change process because the cost of delay is high and the window for failure is narrow. A security patch management workflow builds on the general change practices but adds extra verification steps.

Key activities are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Vulnerability assessment.} Identify the affected component, understand the severity, and decide on the required patch level.
  \item \term{Patch acquisition.} Obtain the official patch package from the vendor, verify its integrity with checksums or signatures, and store it in a controlled repository.
  \item \term{Pre deployment testing.} Deploy the patch to a staging environment that mirrors production. Run regression tests, security scans and performance checks to ensure the patch does not introduce new issues.
  \item \term{Release planning.} Schedule the patch deployment during a low traffic window, communicate the plan to stakeholders, and prepare a rollback artefact.
  \item \term{Post deployment monitoring.} After the patch is live, increase the frequency of relevant alerts, watch for unexpected error spikes and verify that the vulnerability is no longer exploitable.
\end{itemize}

The process emphasizes traceability and rapid verification so that the organization can protect assets without sacrificing stability.

\begin{example}
A financial services platform discovers a critical library vulnerability. The security team downloads the vendor's patch, checks the SHA 256 hash, and stores the file in an internal artefact repository. A dedicated staging cluster is updated with the patched library, and the full test suite plus an additional penetration test are executed. All pass, so the release manager schedules a midnight rollout, updates the runbook with the new steps, and tags the release as v5.1.1 security. After deployment, the monitoring system raises an alert for a brief increase in database latency; the on call engineer reviews the logs, confirms the patch is active, and the alert clears within ten minutes.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Imagine a new security patch must be applied to a live service. List the steps you would take from committing the fix to confirming that the service remains healthy after deployment. Include build, test, deployment, monitoring and rollback considerations.
\end{keytest}

