\section{Teamwork and Ethics}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify the typical roles that appear in a software development team;
  \item describe effective communication practices for collaborative work;
  \item explain the concept of professional responsibility and give examples of ethical behaviour in a technical context;
  \item recognise how teams handle ethical dilemmas and make decisions that affect users and society;
  \item analyse the impact of cultural diversity on team dynamics and ethical choices.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Roles in a team}

A software project rarely relies on a single person. Successful teams combine complementary skills. The most common roles are:

\term{Project manager} -- plans the schedule, allocates resources, monitors progress and reports to stakeholders. The manager keeps the team aligned with the project goals and removes obstacles that could delay delivery.

\term{Business analyst} -- gathers requirements from clients, translates them into functional specifications, and ensures that the final product meets the users' needs. The analyst acts as a bridge between the business side and the technical side.

\term{Designer} -- creates the visual layout, user interface and user experience. The designer decides how the system looks, how information is organised and how users interact with the product.

\term{Developer} -- writes the source code, implements algorithms and integrates components. Developers may specialise in front end, back end or full stack work, but all share responsibility for producing clean, maintainable code.

\term{Tester} -- designs test cases, runs automated and manual tests, and reports defects. Testing verifies that the software behaves as expected and that quality standards are met before release.

\term{DevOps engineer} -- builds and maintains the deployment pipeline, configures servers and ensures that the system can be released reliably and repeatedly. The engineer also monitors performance and handles incidents in production.

Roles are flexible. In small teams one person may fulfil several functions; in large organisations each role may be split into sub roles such as front end developer or security tester. The essential point is that each role contributes a distinct perspective that helps the team deliver a complete, reliable product.

\subsection{Communication}

Effective communication is the glue that holds a team together. It has three main dimensions: information flow, feedback and documentation.

\term{Information flow} -- regular updates keep everyone aware of what has been done and what remains. Daily stand up meetings, weekly sprint reviews and status emails are common mechanisms. The goal is to share progress without overloading the team with unnecessary detail.

\term{Feedback} -- constructive criticism improves the work and prevents misunderstandings. Feedback should be specific, focused on the artefact rather than the person, and delivered promptly. For example, a reviewer might comment, ``The function name does not reflect its purpose; consider renaming it to calculateTax.''

\term{Documentation} -- written records preserve knowledge beyond the life of a meeting. Requirements documents, design diagrams, code comments and test reports all serve as reference points. Good documentation follows a clear structure, uses consistent terminology and is kept up to date.

Communication tools such as issue trackers, version control platforms and instant messaging apps support these practices. Tools are only as effective as the habits of the people using them. Teams should agree on conventions -- for instance, tagging issues with priority levels or using a standard template for pull request descriptions -- and enforce them consistently.

\begin{example}
In a mobile app project the developers use a shared repository on GitHub. Each pull request must include a short description of the change, a reference to the related issue, and a checklist of required tests. The project manager reviews the pull request, adds comments if the code does not meet the style guide, and merges it only after all checks pass. This disciplined communication prevents broken builds and ensures that every change is traceable.
\end{example}

\subsection{Professional responsibility}

Professional responsibility means that a practitioner recognises the impact of their work on users, colleagues and society, and acts accordingly. In software engineering this responsibility appears in several forms.

\term{Quality assurance} -- delivering software that works as advertised, is secure and performs reliably. Cutting corners on testing or ignoring known vulnerabilities breaches professional duty.

\term{Confidentiality} -- respecting the privacy of data entrusted to the team. Developers must follow data protection policies, encrypt sensitive information and avoid exposing credentials in public repositories.

\term{Intellectual property} -- acknowledging the source of third party libraries, complying with licences and not claiming ownership of code that belongs to others. Using an open source component under a copyleft licence without providing the required source code violates legal and ethical standards.

\term{Social impact} -- considering how the software may affect users and the wider community. For example, an algorithm that predicts credit scores should be examined for bias, and a social media feature that encourages addictive use should be designed responsibly.

Professional codes of conduct, such as those published by the ACM or IEEE, codify these expectations. Violations can lead to loss of reputation, legal consequences or harm to end users.

\begin{example}
A developer discovers that a logging module writes user passwords in plain text to a log file. Recognising the security risk, the developer immediately reports the issue to the team lead, proposes a fix that hashes the passwords before logging, and updates the documentation to warn future contributors. This action demonstrates responsibility to both the users and the organisation.
\end{example}

\subsection{Ethical decision making in teams}

Even with clear responsibilities, teams frequently face situations where the right course of action is not obvious. Ethical decision making provides a structured way to evaluate options and choose a path that aligns with professional values.

The process usually follows four steps:

\term{Identify the dilemma} -- state clearly what the conflict is. For instance, a client requests a feature that could collect location data without explicit consent.

\term{Gather relevant information} -- collect facts about legal requirements, user expectations, technical constraints and organisational policies.

\term{Consider stakeholders} -- list who will be affected: end users, the client, the development team, regulators and the broader public.

\term{Choose and act} -- evaluate alternatives against ethical principles such as fairness, transparency and respect for privacy, then decide on the most responsible option and document the reasoning.

\begin{example}
A team is asked to add a shortcut that automatically shares a user's contact list with a third party advertising service. The developers recognise that the shortcut would violate user privacy. Following the decision making process, they identify the dilemma, gather that the service's terms of use require explicit consent, note that users and regulators would be harmed, and decide to refuse the shortcut. Instead they propose a consent driven alternative that lets users opt in voluntarily. The documented decision helps the client understand the ethical rationale and preserves the team's integrity.
\end{example}

\subsection{Cultural diversity and ethics}

Teams often bring together members from different cultural backgrounds. Diversity enriches problem solving but can also create misunderstandings about ethical norms. Awareness of cultural differences helps teams create inclusive policies.

Key points include:

\term{Respect for different communication styles} -- some cultures prefer direct feedback, others value indirect approaches. Teams should establish a shared feedback protocol that accommodates both preferences.

\term{Varied views on data privacy} -- regulations and expectations differ across regions. When a product is deployed internationally, the team must adopt the most stringent privacy standards to avoid ethical lapses.

\term{Inclusive decision making} -- ensure that all voices are heard during ethical deliberations. Rotating the role of decision facilitator can prevent dominance by a single cultural perspective.

\begin{example}
An international team is developing a health tracking app. Members from a jurisdiction with strict health data laws remind the group to implement end to end encryption, even though the primary market has looser rules. By adopting the higher standard, the team respects the most protective cultural expectations and avoids future compliance issues.
\end{example}

\subsection{Conflict resolution in diverse teams}

Conflicts arise when expectations, priorities or values clash. A clear resolution process helps maintain trust and keeps the focus on project goals. The recommended steps are:

\term{State the issue} -- each party describes the problem in factual terms without blame.

\term{Listen actively} -- participants repeat back what they heard to confirm understanding.

\term{Identify common ground} -- highlight objectives that all agree are important, such as user safety or timely delivery.

\term{Explore alternatives} -- brainstorm solutions that address the underlying concerns of each side.

\term{Agree on actions} -- decide on a concrete plan, assign responsibilities and set a follow up time.

Applying this method prevents escalation and ensures that cultural differences are treated as assets rather than obstacles.

\begin{example}
Two developers disagree about the level of logging required for a new feature. One argues for minimal logs to preserve performance; the other worries about insufficient data for troubleshooting. Using the conflict resolution steps, they state their positions, listen to each other, agree that both performance and diagnosability are important, propose a configurable logging level, and document the decision. The compromise satisfies both concerns and respects the team's collaborative culture.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Imagine you are joining a new software project. Ask yourself:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Which role best matches my current skills, and what additional responsibilities will I need to learn?
  \item How will I keep the team informed about my progress, and how will I receive feedback?
  \item What ethical issues might arise from the product we are building, and how can I help address them?
  \item When faced with a conflict between a client request and user privacy, what steps will I take to reach an ethical decision?
  \item How can I ensure that cultural differences in my team do not lead to ethical oversights?
  \item If a disagreement arises within the team, what process will I follow to resolve it constructively?
\end{itemize}
If you can answer these questions clearly, you are ready to work responsibly in a team.
\end{keytest}

